.Dd August 25, 2026
.Dt PORTIA 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm portia
.Nd AI-driven FreeBSD porter's assistant
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm new
.Ar port
.Nm
.Cm update
.Ar port
.Nm
.Cm troubleshoot
.Ar port
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a deterministic harness around a language model that automates the
mechanical parts of porting software to FreeBSD.
It does no porting itself: it owns the command, the target directory,
the prompt handed to the model, and the check that a port-shaped result
landed.
Only the porting work in the middle
\(em reading the distfile, matching dependencies, writing the Makefile
\(em is left to the model.
.Pp
Each command takes a single
.Ar port
argument, resolved before the model runs into an origin, a ports tree,
and a port directory.
It is one of two forms:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar category/port
An origin: a single slash with non-empty halves, taken relative to
.Ev PORTSDIR .
The port directory is
.Pa category/port
beneath
.Ev PORTSDIR ,
which is the ports tree.
.It Ar path/to/tree/category/port
A path to a port directory inside a ports tree.
The origin is its last two components and the ports tree is the path
above them;
.Ev PORTSDIR
is not consulted.
A trailing slash, such as one left by shell tab completion, is ignored.
.El
.Pp
An argument with no
.Pa category/port
tail is neither form and is rejected.
.Nm
creates the port directory, hands the model the command's prompt
together with the origin and the target path, and then verifies the
expected port files landed there non-empty.
.Pp
The ports tree must be a repository managed by jj or git.
.Nm
prefers jj when both are present, so the commit can be named by a stable
jj change id rather than a git sha, and because a jj workspace has no
git directory of its own.
Once the expected files are present,
.Nm
commits the port when the port directory has uncommitted changes, using
a message the model writes to a scratch file it is handed.
The message follows the commit conventions of the surrounding tree and,
for an
.Cm update ,
cites the upstream changelog for every intermediate version.
It also records any dependency constraint the model had to loosen
because no port in the tree provides the version upstream requires.
Committing is keyed on the port directory, not on the run: uncommitted
changes
\(em including a complete port left on disk by an interrupted earlier
run
\(em are committed, while a port already at its desired state, with
nothing uncommitted, is a success that leaves the tree untouched.
.Nm
refuses to run at all when the ports tree is under neither jj nor git.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width troubleshoot
.It Cm new Ar port
Create a new, complete port.
Free-form detail
.Pq version, home page, author, git repository
is read from standard input and passed to the model as context.
From it the model finds the distfile, determines the build system and
dependencies, mines the ports tree for prior art, and produces a
.Pa Makefile ,
a
.Pa distinfo ,
and a
.Pa pkg-descr .
Where the distfile ships a test suite, the model hooks it into the
port's
.Cm test
target.
.It Cm update Ar port
Bump an existing port to its newest upstream version.
The model raises the version, resets
.Va PORTREVISION ,
reconciles dependencies and their version constraints, and regenerates
.Pa distinfo
for the new distfile.
It also keeps the port's
.Cm test
target running the new release's suite, and adds one where upstream
ships tests the port never wired up.
.It Cm troubleshoot Ar port
Diagnose and fix a build failure in an existing port.
The model is pointed at the poudriere build logs published under
.Ev POUDRIERE_URL ,
navigates them to the port's most recent failure, researches the error
against the ports tree and the wider FreeBSD sources, and implements a
fix
\(em typically migrating a broken or outdated mechanism to the one
comparable ports use now.
The result is again a
.Pa Makefile ,
.Pa distinfo ,
and
.Pa pkg-descr .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width POUDRIERE_URL
.It Ev MODEL
The model command, defaulting to
.Ql claude .
It is left unquoted at the call site, so additional words split into
separate arguments.
Because the model must be allowed to write files, invoke it with a
permission mode that permits edits:
.Pp
.Dl MODEL="claude --permission-mode acceptEdits" portia new www/foo
.It Ev PORTSDIR
The root of the ports tree for an origin argument, defaulting to the
current directory.
The port is written to
.Pa PORTSDIR/category/port
and committed there once verified.
A path argument carries its own tree, so
.Ev PORTSDIR
is not consulted then.
Either way the ports tree must be a jj or git repository, and when it
is managed by both the commit is made with jj.
.It Ev POUDRIERE_URL
The base URL of the poudriere instance whose build logs
.Cm troubleshoot
hands the model, defaulting to
.Ql https://poudriere.p7x.dev .
The per-package logs are expected under
.Pa data/latest-per-pkg/
beneath it.
Only
.Cm troubleshoot
consults it.
.El
.Sh FILES
Relative to the port directory:
.Bl -tag -width pkg-descr
.It Pa Makefile
The port Makefile.
.It Pa distinfo
Distfile checksums and sizes, produced by
.Cm new
and
.Cm update .
.It Pa pkg-descr
A prose description of the software.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 when every expected file is present non-empty in the port
directory and the port is either committed or already at its desired
state with nothing to commit.
It exits 64 when invoked with no command or no
.Ar port
argument, and 1 when the argument is neither an origin nor a port path,
a prompt cannot be read, the ports tree is under neither jj nor git, the
model invocation fails, the model returns without producing an expected
file, the port directory has uncommitted changes but no commit message
was written, or the commit fails.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create a new port, feeding it detail on standard input:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
MODEL="claude --permission-mode acceptEdits" \e
    portia new www/foo <<-EOF
	version 1.2.3
	home page https://example.org/foo
	author A. Hacker
	git https://github.com/example/foo
EOF
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr git 1 ,
.Xr jj 1 ,
.Xr sclr 1
.Sh CAVEATS
The porting work is performed by the model and is not guaranteed
correct.
.Nm
and the model it drives work by static analysis alone
\(em fetching and extracting distfiles, reading the sources
\(em and never build the port.
.Nm
verifies only that the expected files were produced; that the port
actually builds must be checked out of band by an operator.
A
.Cm test
target is likewise wired up from a reading of the distfile and never
run.
